It's strange how much easier it gets not to make certain mistakes after making them just once. You'd think a mistake that was easy to avoid making twice wouldn't be too too difficult to avoid making even once, but that's not usually how it works.

The good news is, if some domain has a finite number of possible mistakes and you never make mistakes twice, you have a chance of approaching perfection eventually.

As Bohr said, "An expert is a man who has made all the mistakes which can be made in a very narrow field."

